{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "120",
    "case_summary": "A patient with high myopia was examined. The axial length was 30.0 mm.\n\nOphthalmoscopic examination revealed a yellowish oval lesion within a wide area of chorioretinal atrophy.\n\n- Infrared examination: Showed a ringlike low reflectance pattern (Figure A)- Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography:  Image Title: Enhanced depth imaging spectral-domain OCT  Image Description: Revealed a focal scleral ectasia with no overlying retina. The sclera was approximately one-third thinner (98, 77, and 50 μm for the pit's floor, temporal wall, and nasal wall, respectively). The choroid was obliterated. Width and depth of 522 × 536 μm.- Autofluorescence imaging: The pit had a homogeneous low autofluorescence- Fluorescein angiography: Showed a branch of a posterior ciliary artery near the lesion. The pit was slightly hyperfluorescent in the late frames.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Myopic Chorioretinal Atrophy\nB. Myopic Maculopathy with Chorioretinal Atrophy\nC. Myopic macular pit\nD. Posterior staphyloma (pathologic myopia)\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Chronic Progressive Ocular Syndrome with Chorioretinal Atrophy and Scleral Ectasia",
